<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert of Brandenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1490–1545</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Albrecht of Mainz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> was a Hohenzollern prince who became Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, administrator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> and, in 1514, Archbishop-Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, and was made a cardinal in 1518. To pay the debts from acquiring these sees he promoted the sale of indulgences, which provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s Ninety-five Theses in 1517.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Albert of Brandenburg" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinal_Albrecht_of_Brandenburg_(DE_SPSG_GKI10219).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
